@flexiarg process-coherence/refusal-becomes-pattern
@title Turn an Underdetermined Implementation Refusal into a Pattern
@keywords implementation refusal, underdetermination, evidence before ruling, warrant, next steps, operator gate
@audience builders facing several conformant implementations when recorded evidence cannot yet distinguish them
@references [orchestration/pattern-warranted-choice-point]
@tone procedural
@style pattern
@provenance Joe ruling, 2026-09-08; futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/RULINGS-walkthrough-2026-09-08.md:39-58

! conclusion: When an interface has several conformant implementations and the available record cannot decide among them, replace the implementation refusal with a written pattern that warrants one evidence-producing implementation. Keep the unresolved choices explicit, and use the implementation's next-step evidence to support or revise the pattern before deciding them.

  + context:
      A required interface is specified, its laws and candidate implementations have been
      exercised, and more than one implementation remains conformant. The F11 find interface
      is the canonical instance: its refusal says that the recorded F1-F4 instances do not
      select one canonical implementation
      (futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/aif-equations.edn:812-818).

  + IF:
      Progress is blocked on an operator-shaped choice, but the operator would be choosing
      between implementations without the operational evidence that distinguishes their
      consequences. For F11, the receipt-carrier and falsifiability-reading choices remain
      observed-not-decided until implementation evidence arrives
      (futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/RULINGS-walkthrough-2026-09-08.md:52-55).

  + HOWEVER:
      Leaving the refusal in place prevents the runs that could produce the missing evidence;
      asking the operator to decide first turns an evidence question into an unsupported
      preference. The F11 ruling identifies this explicitly: waiting on the operator serialises
      evidence gathering that can proceed under a written warrant
      (futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/RULINGS-walkthrough-2026-09-08.md:55-58).

  + THEN:
      Write down the refusal's grounds as a pattern. Use that pattern as the warrant for one
      implementation that satisfies the stated interface and laws. Record the implementation's
      consequences as NEXT STEPS against every unresolved choice; do not silently settle those
      choices in the implementation. Support, amend, or refuse the pattern when that evidence
      is available. In the F11 sequence, the pattern is written first, find is implemented
      second, and evidence deciding the receipt carrier and F4 reading is gathered third
      (futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:1401-1404).

  + BECAUSE:
      A refusal is useful when it prevents an unwarranted commitment, but it becomes circular
      when it also prevents the observation needed to warrant any commitment. A written pattern
      preserves the reason for caution while making the next experiment auditable: the builder
      can point to the warrant, the unresolved choices stay visible, and the resulting evidence
      can change the pattern instead of being mistaken for a prior ruling. Joe ruled this
      disposition for F11 and stated that the experience would be more useful than a ruling
      made before the implementation existed
      (futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/RULINGS-walkthrough-2026-09-08.md:41-47).

    + evidence:
        F11's ruling is recorded as :find-sorry :status :decided with the dependent choices
        deliberately left observed-not-decided
        (futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/aif-equations.edn:812-818).

    + COUNTERFACTUAL:
        Refuse this pattern when the candidate implementations cannot satisfy the stated laws,
        when the action is irreversible or outside the authority already granted, or when the
        proposed run cannot distinguish any unresolved choice. In those cases an implementation
        would generate no deciding evidence, so this warrant does not apply.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
      - Implement F11 find under this warrant without recording a receipt-carrier or F4 ruling.
      - Record which implementation observations distinguish the open receipt-carrier arms.
      - Record which implementation observations distinguish the open F4 readings.
      - Revisit this pattern and the two choice entries with those records, preserving any
        result that remains "not found" as such.
